## Multi-Timeframe Extension

Run the same regression on 3 windows:

```
Short (20 bars):  slope_s, R²_s, noise_s, t_s
Medium (60 bars): slope_m, R²_m, noise_m, t_m
Long (200 bars):  slope_l, R²_l, noise_l, t_l
```

**Read the pattern:**

| Short | Long | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| High R², +slope | High R², +slope | Aligned uptrend — best trade |
| High R², +slope | Low R² | Short-term pop in range — fade |
| Low R² | High R², +slope | Pullback in uptrend — buy dip |
| Low R² | Low R² | Dead — avoid |

This is how you solve the fractal problem **with regression instead of ER**.

---

## Why Regression Beats Simple Slope

- **Simple slope** ignores noise → false signals
- **Regression slope** accounts for noise via SE
- **R²** separates trend from chop automatically
- **t-stat** filters luck from real trends
- **Residual std** gives you stop size directly

One regression → **four tradeable numbers.** No clustering, no arbitrary buckets.

---

## Critical Rules

1. **Always normalize** slope and residual std by price → comparable across stocks.
2. **Use log prices** for long windows → slope becomes % return per bar.
3. **R² is your trend filter** — nothing else needed to separate trending from sideways.
4. **t-stat > 2 is your entry gate** — don't trade unconfirmed slopes.
5. **Residual std is your stop-loss** — set stop at 2× residual std from the line.

---

## One-Line Summary

**Slope = direction & speed. R² = trend vs noise. Std of residuals = how wide the noise is (stop size). Std error / t-stat = is the trend real. Four numbers from one regression replace your entire classification system.**

Run this on 3 timeframes and you have a complete, noise-robust, fractal-aware signature.